Ze-Reviewed-Against: github.com/vishvananda/netlink v1.3.1

selFromPolicy sends a non-zero XfrmPolicy.SrcPortMask or DstPortMask as
given, in network byte order. Upstream derives each selector mask from its
port alone, all ones for a non-zero port and zero for port 0, so neither an
exact port 0 nor a mask-aligned block can be asked for. The kernel matches a
selector port as (flow ^ sel) & mask (__xfrm4_selector_match,
net/xfrm/xfrm_policy.c) and reads an ICMP type into the source port and its
code into the destination port (xfrm_flowi_sport, xfrm_flowi_dport,
include/net/xfrm.h), so the
upstream rule cannot express ICMP type 0 (Echo Reply) exactly, nor a code
range as the aligned blocks that cover it. RFC 4301 Section 4.4.1.1: "This
16-bit selector can contain a single type and a range of codes, a single type
and ANY code, and ANY type and ANY code." A zero mask keeps the upstream
derivation, so every caller that sets no mask is unchanged. XfrmPolicy gains
DstPortMask and SrcPortMask, which the policy reader and the reader of a
state's nested selector fill from the kernel.

No upstream pull request is opened yet for this change.

Needs netlink-xfrm-04-readback-message-family.patch, and so
netlink-xfrm-01-state-readback-esn-replay.patch: its state hunk realigns
the nested selector literal that 04 rewrites.

Not yet checked against upstream main.

Delete this patch when go.mod requires an upstream release that contains the
change.

diff --git a/vendor/github.com/vishvananda/netlink/xfrm_policy_linux.go b/vendor/github.com/vishvananda/netlink/xfrm_policy_linux.go
--- a/vendor/github.com/vishvananda/netlink/xfrm_policy_linux.go
+++ b/vendor/github.com/vishvananda/netlink/xfrm_policy_linux.go
@@ -93,6 +93,12 @@
 	Ifid     int
 	Mark     *XfrmMark
 	Tmpls    []XfrmPolicyTmpl
+	// Selector port masks, in host byte order. The reader fills them from the
+	// kernel. The writer sends a non-zero mask as given; a zero mask keeps the
+	// upstream rule of deriving the mask from the port value (any port for 0,
+	// exact for any other value).
+	DstPortMask uint16
+	SrcPortMask uint16
 }
 
 func (p XfrmPolicy) String() string {
@@ -122,6 +128,16 @@
 	if sel.Sport != 0 {
 		sel.SportMask = ^uint16(0)
 	}
+	// An explicit mask wins over the derived one. The kernel matches
+	// (flow ^ sel) & mask, so a caller can ask for exactly port 0, or for a
+	// mask-aligned block such as an ICMP code range (the kernel reads an ICMP
+	// type into the source port and its code into the destination port).
+	if policy.DstPortMask != 0 {
+		sel.DportMask = nl.Swap16(policy.DstPortMask)
+	}
+	if policy.SrcPortMask != 0 {
+		sel.SportMask = nl.Swap16(policy.SrcPortMask)
+	}
 	sel.Ifindex = int32(policy.Ifindex)
 }
 
@@ -352,6 +368,8 @@
 	policy.Proto = Proto(msg.Sel.Proto)
 	policy.DstPort = int(nl.Swap16(msg.Sel.Dport))
 	policy.SrcPort = int(nl.Swap16(msg.Sel.Sport))
+	policy.DstPortMask = nl.Swap16(msg.Sel.DportMask)
+	policy.SrcPortMask = nl.Swap16(msg.Sel.SportMask)
 	policy.Ifindex = int(msg.Sel.Ifindex)
 	policy.Priority = int(msg.Priority)
 	policy.Index = int(msg.Index)
diff --git a/vendor/github.com/vishvananda/netlink/xfrm_state_linux.go b/vendor/github.com/vishvananda/netlink/xfrm_state_linux.go
--- a/vendor/github.com/vishvananda/netlink/xfrm_state_linux.go
